@@ -0,0 +1,31 @@
+---
+id: dl-002-rental-periods-are-whole-utc-days-and-booking-ids-are-sequential
+type: decision-log
+title: "Rental periods are whole UTC days and booking ids are sequential"
+status: pending
+format: 1
+---
+
+# Context
+
+The school's rules fix three things about a rental: money is an integer number of euro cents, times
+are UTC ISO-8601 strings, and a period covers whole days from midnight UTC to the start of the day
+after its last day. The rules leave open how strictly the input strings are checked, how the price
+is computed, and how booking ids are made.
+
+# Decision
+
+- A period boundary must be a UTC midnight written as `YYYY-MM-DDT00:00:00Z`, with an optional
+  `.000` before the `Z`. Any other form is rejected. The strings are returned as they were given.
+- The price is the number of days times the item's `dailyPrice`, which is an integer number of
+  cents. A `dailyPrice` that is not a non-negative safe integer is rejected when the item is added.
+- Booking ids come from a counter that starts at 1 and are written `booking-<n>`. They do not use
+  the clock or randomness, in line with the determinism rule.
+
+# Consequences
+
+- The price is exact: no floating-point rounding is involved.
+- The strict format rejects offsets such as `+01:00` and times other than midnight. A client that
+  sends local times must convert them to UTC midnight first.
+- Ids depend only on the order of bookings in one service instance. A new service starts again at
+  `booking-1`, so ids are unique only within one service.
diff --git a/docs/memory/decision-log/dl-003-item-ids-are-unique-and-quotes-do-not-check-availability.md b/docs/memory/decision-log/dl-003-item-ids-are-unique-and-quotes-do-not-check-availability.md
